<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ronald Webster</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1926">1926</a>–<a href="/years/2016">2016</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ronald Webster was the leader of the Anguillan revolution against rule from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kitts">Saint Kitts</a>. After islanders expelled the Kittitian police in 1967, he headed the island council and in 1969 proclaimed the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anguilla">Anguilla</a>, which led to a British landing. He later served as chief minister when Anguilla became a separate British dependency.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>James Ronald Webster was born on 2 March 1926 in Island Harbour, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anguilla">Anguilla</a>, one of eight children who survived infancy out of sixteen. For 27 years he worked at a dairy farm and factory on Saint Martin, whose owners treated him as a son and left him their whole estate. When he returned to Anguilla in 1960 he found the island without electricity, paved streets or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">telephones</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1967 Anguillans forced the officials and police of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kitts">Saint Kitts</a> off the island, citing mistreatment of the public and misuse of government funds, and Webster, who led the revolt, became chairman of the Anguilla Island Council. In a referendum on 11 July the islanders voted overwhelmingly to leave the Associated State and become a separate British colony. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> sent an adviser, Tony Lee, to share an interim administrative authority with Webster from January 1968 to January 1969, but Saint Kitts refused to extend the arrangement and the British left. In February 1969 the islanders voted again to remain apart and become an independent republic, with Webster leading the council.</p><p>In March 1969 the British junior minister William Whitlock arrived to arrange another interim agreement and was expelled within hours. Eight days later 315 British paratroopers and two frigates landed to restore order, Tony Lee was installed as commissioner, and Webster left the island.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>After an interim agreement in 1971 and a new constitution in 1976, Webster became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anguilla">Anguilla</a>'s first Chief Minister when his People's Progressive Party won the election of 1976, serving from 10 February 1976 until a no-confidence vote backed by his own ministers removed him in 1977. After Anguilla was formally separated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kitts-and-nevis">Saint Kitts and Nevis</a> in 1980 he returned as Chief Minister and held office until his defeat in 1984. He died on 9 December 2016, and his birthday has been a public holiday in Anguilla since 2010.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ronald Webster” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ronald Webster</b> emerged as the leader of the movement.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-anguilla">Republic of Anguilla</a></li></ul></section>
